Very often "NoClassDefFoundError" masks the underlying class loading error - so if you know for sure that you have that class in your JAR file (or on the class path - check .jclassPath()) the cause is usually JavaVM version mismatch - i.e. a class compiled on 1.6 won't work in 1.5. You have to use -target 1.4 -source 1.4 (or 1.5 etc.) when compiling the classes and make sure there is not incompatible one.

You can find out the version easily using the "file" utility on the command line:

Fino:REngine$ file REXP.class 
REXP.class: compiled Java class data, version 48.0 (Java 1.4)

Note that OS X comes with several JVM versions check R to see which is being used.

If that doesn't solve the problem, let us know - there are debugging options you can enable in rJava to see what the loader does.

Cheers,
Simon

