> I see that rJava has two seperated folders, i386 and x64. Whay can't rJava find and use the right jri.dll? Any way to do this automatically?
> 

It's a little complicated because Java doesn't know about R's arch settings (until JRI is loaded but that's too late). If you tell Java about the r_arch setting, it works:

> library(rJava)
> .jinit(parameters=paste("-Dr.arch=/",.Platform$r_arch,sep=''))
> .jengine(T)
[1] "Java-Object{Thread[Thread-0,5,main]}"

I could have .jinit() set r.arch automatically, but normally that is the responsibility of the invoker (e.g., JGR launcher sets r.arch correctly when starting Java that will host JRI). In general, starting JRI from R is very dangerous and not supported on most platforms, so I'm little reluctant to add that to rJava in general.

Cheers,
Simon


